menu "Home CSI node"

comment "Per-node identity and secrets live in NVS, not here."
comment "Everything below is a BENCH FALLBACK used only when the NVS key is absent."

config HCS_ALLOW_KCONFIG_FALLBACK
    bool "Allow Kconfig fallbacks when an NVS key is missing"
    default y
    help
      When enabled, a node whose NVS has not been provisioned will fall back
      to the compile-time values in this menu. That is convenient on the
      bench and dangerous in the field, because it lets an unprovisioned
      board come up with a default (or empty) PSK.

      Turn this OFF for production images: the node will then refuse to
      start the uplink unless NVS supplies node_id, PSK, server and Wi-Fi
      credentials, and will say exactly which key is missing.

menu "Identity fallback (bench only)"

config HCS_DEFAULT_NODE_ID
    int "Fallback node_id (1-65535, 0 is invalid)"
    range 0 65535
    default 0
    help
      0 means "not set": the node will refuse to send. Set a real id here
      only for bench work. Production nodes get this from NVS
      (tools/provision.py).

config HCS_DEFAULT_PSK_HEX
    string "Fallback 32-byte PSK as 64 hex chars (EMPTY = none)"
    default ""
    help
      LEAVE THIS EMPTY. A key committed to a Kconfig file is not a secret.
      It exists only so a developer can run a bench node against a local
      server without provisioning NVS first. If it is empty the node will
      not send anything.

      Reusing one key across nodes breaks the nonce-uniqueness argument in
      docs/protocol.md section 4, so never use this on more than one board
      at a time.

endmenu

menu "Network fallback (bench only)"

config HCS_DEFAULT_AP_SSID
    string "Fallback dedicated-AP SSID"
    default ""

config HCS_DEFAULT_AP_PASSWORD
    string "Fallback dedicated-AP password"
    default ""

config HCS_DEFAULT_SERVER_HOST
    string "Fallback ingest host (DNS name or IP)"
    default ""

config HCS_DEFAULT_SERVER_PORT
    int "Fallback ingest UDP port"
    range 1 65535
    default 5566

config HCS_DEFAULT_CHANNEL
    int "Pinned 2.4 GHz channel (1-14)"
    range 1 14
    default 6
    help
      MUST match the dedicated AP's fixed channel. All nodes and the AP live
      on this one channel at 20 MHz; see docs/architecture.md.

config HCS_DEFAULT_SNTP_SERVER
    string "SNTP server"
    default "pool.ntp.org"

endmenu

menu "Capture and sounding"

config HCS_SOUNDING_INTERVAL_MS
    int "Broadcast sounding interval (ms)"
    range 20 60000
    default 100
    help
      Each node broadcasts one tiny sounding frame every interval, so every
      other node on the channel gets a CSI sample of that link for free.
      100 ms = 10 Hz per node: 9 nodes is ~90 tiny frames/s on the channel,
      negligible airtime, and 10 samples/s per directional link is ample for
      human motion (roughly 0.5-5 Hz Doppler).

config HCS_SOUNDING_JITTER_PCT
    int "Sounding schedule jitter (percent)"
    range 0 90
    default 25
    help
      Nodes flashed from the same image with the same interval would
      otherwise lock into synchronised transmissions and collide forever.
      Each interval is multiplied by a random factor in
      [1 - pct/100, 1 + pct/100].

config HCS_RSSI_FLOOR_DBM
    int "Ignore captured frames weaker than this (dBm)"
    range -100 0
    default -85
    help
      Bounds the amount of unusable far-field noise that reaches the ring.

config HCS_MAC_ALLOWLIST_ENFORCED
    bool "Only keep CSI from allowlisted source MACs"
    default n
    help
      When on, only frames whose source MAC is in the NVS allowlist (the
      other nodes) or the associated AP produce records. Foreign household
      traffic is dropped at the callback. Useful once the mesh is proven and
      you want a clean, bounded stream.

config HCS_CSI_MAX_LEN
    int "Maximum raw CSI bytes kept per record"
    range 64 1103
    default 384
    help
      UNVERIFIED HARDWARE VALUE, on every target this firmware builds for.
      The ESP32 family reports CSI buffers whose length depends on the
      decoded format; 384 covers LLTF+HT-LTF at 20 MHz on the classic ESP32
      according to public documentation. On the primary target, ESP32-C6,
      docs/hardware-esp32c6.md notes Espressif's own figures point to a
      SHORTER typical record (~53 usable HT20 subcarriers), so 384 should
      still be a safe ceiling there too - but "should" is not "measured" on
      either board. Anything longer than this value is dropped and counted
      (never truncated). Run firmware/bringup/csi-hello on your actual board
      and set this from the `len` values it prints.

config HCS_RING_SLOTS
    int "CSI ring buffer slots"
    range 8 512
    default 64
    help
      RAM cost is roughly slots * (HCS_CSI_MAX_LEN + 32) bytes, statically
      allocated. 64 * 416 = ~26 kB.

endmenu

menu "Uplink and budgets"

config HCS_MAX_RECORDS_PER_BATCH
    int "max_records_per_batch (docs/protocol.md S11)"
    range 1 64
    default 16

config HCS_FLUSH_BUDGET_MS
    int "flush_time_budget_ms (docs/protocol.md S11)"
    range 10 10000
    default 200

config HCS_HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL_MS
    int "Heartbeat interval (ms)"
    range 1000 600000
    default 15000

config HCS_BW_SOUNDING_RPS
    int "Sounding-derived records per second cap"
    range 0 2000
    default 50

config HCS_BW_FOREIGN_RPS
    int "Foreign-frame records per second cap (0 disables foreign capture)"
    range 0 2000
    default 5

config HCS_BW_BYTES_PER_SEC
    int "Overall record byte-rate cap (bytes/s, 0 = unlimited)"
    range 0 1000000
    default 32768

config HCS_DECIMATE_FOREIGN_START_PCT
    int "Ring occupancy at which foreign records start being thinned"
    range 0 100
    default 40

config HCS_DECIMATE_SOUNDING_START_PCT
    int "Ring occupancy at which sounding records start being thinned"
    range 0 100
    default 70

config HCS_DECIMATE_FULL_PCT
    int "Ring occupancy at which the maximum decimation divisor applies"
    range 0 100
    default 95

config HCS_DECIMATE_MAX_DIVISOR
    int "Maximum decimation divisor (1 disables decimation)"
    range 1 64
    default 8

config HCS_RECONNECT_REBOOT_S
    int "Reboot if Wi-Fi has been down this long (seconds, 0 = never)"
    range 0 86400
    default 900

endmenu

menu "Realtime (burst) mode from GET /device/mode"

comment "main/mode_client.c polls the device API for a temporary 'run hot'"
comment "directive (docs/device-api.md, brief B1). Every ceiling below is"
comment "enforced ON THIS NODE, independent of anything the server says -"
comment "a server value beyond one of these is clamped to it and logged,"
comment "never applied verbatim and never rejected outright."

config HCS_MODE_POLL_MIN_INTERVAL_S
    int "Floor on how often this node will poll /device/mode (seconds)"
    range 1 3600
    default 10
    help
      A server-proposed pollIntervalS below this is clamped up to it. Also
      used as the bootstrap poll cadence before the first successful
      response. Protects the device API from being hammered by a fast or
      misconfigured poll interval; it has no bearing on how quickly a
      realtime window ends, because that is enforced locally (see
      HCS_REALTIME_MAX_DURATION_S below) and never depends on polling.

config HCS_REALTIME_MIN_SOUNDING_INTERVAL_MS
    int "Floor on the sounding interval (ms) while in realtime mode"
    range 5 60000
    default 20
    help
      A server-proposed soundingIntervalMs below this is clamped up to it.
      This is the same knob as HCS_SOUNDING_INTERVAL_MS above, just with an
      independent, tighter floor that applies only to what a server may
      request - a buggy or hostile server can make this node sound at up to
      50 Hz (this default), never faster, no matter what it asks for.

config HCS_REALTIME_MAX_SOUNDING_RPS
    int "Ceiling on the sounding-class record admission rate (records/s)"
    range 1 2000
    default 200
    help
      A server-proposed soundingRps above this is clamped down to it. This
      is the realtime-mode ceiling on the same admission-rate knob as
      HCS_BW_SOUNDING_RPS above (bw_budget.c, BW_CLASS_SOUNDING) - a buggy or
      hostile server cannot make this node admit CSI records faster than
      this, regardless of what it asks for.

config HCS_REALTIME_MIN_FLUSH_BUDGET_MS
    int "Floor on flush_time_budget_ms while in realtime mode"
    range 5 10000
    default 20
    help
      A server-proposed flushBudgetMs below this is clamped up to it. Too
      low a flush budget means one UDP datagram per CSI record instead of a
      batch - more airtime and CPU per record, for no sensing benefit.

config HCS_REALTIME_MAX_RECORDS_PER_BATCH
    int "Ceiling on max_records_per_batch while in realtime mode"
    range 1 64
    default 64
    help
      A server-proposed maxRecordsPerBatch above this is clamped down to it.
      64 is also the hard upper bound of HCS_MAX_RECORDS_PER_BATCH above, so
      by default this ceiling does not add a tighter limit on its own - it
      exists so a deployment that wants a stricter realtime-only cap has
      somewhere to set it without touching the normal-mode default.

config HCS_REALTIME_MAX_DURATION_S
    int "Ceiling on how long a single realtime (burst) window may run"
    range 60 86400
    default 3600
    help
      THE load-bearing ceiling. main/mode_client.c converts the server's
      `expiresAt` into a deadline on this node's own esp_timer clock at the
      moment realtime mode is applied, and the window length used is always
      clamped to this many seconds - regardless of what expiresAt claims,
      regardless of this node's wall clock (synced or not), and regardless
      of whether the server answers another poll before the window ends.
      This is what makes it impossible for a wedged experiment, or a server
      that has gone away entirely, to leave the mesh running hot forever:
      the node reverts to normal on its own clock no later than this many
      seconds after it last applied a realtime directive, with no server
      round trip required to do so.

endmenu

menu "OTA auto-update"

comment "OTA is off unless the NVS key 'api_base' holds an https:// URL."
comment "These are timings only; they do not enable or disable anything."

config HCS_OTA_START_DELAY_S
    int "Wait this long after boot before the first manifest check (seconds)"
    range 0 86400
    default 120
    help
      A node that has just come up has more useful things to do, and after a
      power cut the whole mesh boots within a second of each other - an
      immediate check would have every node hit the server at once.

config HCS_OTA_CHECK_INTERVAL_S
    int "Manifest check interval (seconds)"
    range 60 604800
    default 3600
    help
      How often the node asks /device/ota/manifest whether there is anything
      for it. Hourly is plenty: a rollout is measured in hours of soak time,
      not minutes. After a failed update attempt this interval is backed off
      (up to 16x), because every attempt erases and rewrites the inactive
      flash slot.

config HCS_OTA_HELLO_INTERVAL_S
    int "Device hello (telemetry) interval (seconds)"
    range 30 86400
    default 300
    help
      POST /device/hello reports firmware version, boot epoch, uptime and OTA
      state. It is what makes a staged rollout observable. It is pure
      telemetry: a failure is a logged warning, and it is deliberately NOT
      part of the rollback health checkpoint (see main/ota.c).

endmenu

menu "Local debug mode"

config HCS_DEBUG_UART
    bool "Print a line per captured CSI record to UART"
    default n
    help
      Field-debugging aid: makes a node observable with nothing but a serial
      cable, no VPS involved. Costs UART time inside the uplink task (never
      inside the CSI callback), so leave it off for real recording.

config HCS_DEBUG_UDP
    bool "Also mirror records to a plaintext UDP endpoint on the LAN"
    default n
    help
      Sends the *unencrypted* batch plaintext to a host on the local
      network so you can watch a node without the VPS chain. This is a
      debugging backdoor around the confidentiality guarantee of
      docs/protocol.md section 5 - never enable it on a deployed node.

config HCS_DEBUG_UDP_HOST
    string "Debug mirror host"
    depends on HCS_DEBUG_UDP
    default "192.168.1.2"

config HCS_DEBUG_UDP_PORT
    int "Debug mirror UDP port"
    depends on HCS_DEBUG_UDP
    range 1 65535
    default 5556

endmenu

menu "Status LED"

choice HCS_LED_BACKEND
    prompt "Status LED backend"
    default HCS_LED_BACKEND_NONE
    help
      THIS PROJECT HAS NOT CONFIRMED THE LED HARDWARE ON EITHER BOARD IT
      TARGETS. On the legacy Halocode (ESP32), the board is documented as
      having a 12-LED RGB ring but neither the driver chip nor the GPIO it
      hangs off was ever confirmed here. On the primary target, ESP32-C6,
      the defaults below (GPIO 8, a single WS2812) are Espressif's own
      documented value for their ESP32-C6-DevKitC-1 reference board's
      onboard addressable LED - a real published fact, not a guess in the
      way the old Halocode default was, but it is still unconfirmed FOR
      YOUR SPECIFIC BOARDS: a bare module, a custom carrier PCB, or any C6
      board that is not that exact reference design will have a different
      pin or no onboard LED at all. The default backend is therefore still
      "none" either way: the firmware logs its state instead of driving
      anything, and every other subsystem works identically regardless of
      this choice.

      Once you have confirmed the real pin for your actual boards, pick a
      backend below.

config HCS_LED_BACKEND_NONE
    bool "None (log state transitions only)"

config HCS_LED_BACKEND_GPIO
    bool "Single plain GPIO (blink patterns)"

config HCS_LED_BACKEND_WS2812
    bool "WS2812-style addressable ring over RMT"

endchoice

config HCS_LED_GPIO
    int "LED data GPIO"
    depends on !HCS_LED_BACKEND_NONE
    range 0 39
    default 8
    help
      Espressif's ESP32-C6-DevKitC-1 reference board wires its one onboard
      addressable (WS2812) LED to GPIO8 - that is where this default comes
      from, and it is correct if your C6 boards ARE that reference design.
      If they are a different C6 board/module (or you are building for the
      legacy esp32 target, where the old Halocode default here was 25 and
      was never more than a guess), this is UNVERIFIED for your hardware:
      confirm with a scope, the board's own schematic, or by trial before
      believing it.

config HCS_LED_COUNT
    int "Number of addressable LEDs"
    depends on HCS_LED_BACKEND_WS2812
    range 1 64
    default 1
    help
      1 matches the single onboard WS2812 on Espressif's ESP32-C6-DevKitC-1
      reference board. The legacy Halocode's documented (also unverified)
      12-LED ring used 12 here instead - set this from what your actual
      board has.

config HCS_LED_MAX_BRIGHTNESS
    int "Maximum per-channel brightness (0-255)"
    depends on !HCS_LED_BACKEND_NONE
    range 1 255
    default 24
    help
      These nodes live in bedrooms and hallways. 24/255 is clearly visible
      in a dark room without being a nightlight.

endmenu

endmenu
